Buffalo Kloof carries rhino and cheetah reintroduction and anti-poaching
work. That kind of operation depends on field communications: camera and
sensor backhaul, ranger contact after dark, and knowing a link has dropped
before it matters. On most reserves that ends up as a patchwork of LTE
routers and whatever signal reaches the lodge.

I'd like to run a desk study on Buffalo Kloof from terrain and public
infrastructure data: where the usable backbone corridors are across the
property, and whether the nearest tower can carry a reliable uplink. It
tells you what is feasible before anyone spends money.

If that's useful, the next step is a half-day assessment on site: what you
have, what fails, what it costs you, and what an owned network would look
like. R3,500 ex VAT, credited against the build, and the report is yours
either way. The one-page proposal is attached; the short version is here:
